## Meal structure

- A fixed protein core: the protein amount is the same at every portion.
- Three portions that change only carbs and fat (rice, pasta, bread, cheese, oil, sauce): L Light, S Standard, X Loaded, each with kcal, protein, carbs, fat, AUD cost per serve and a one-line `what`.
- Fields: `id` (lowercase letters and digits, starting with a letter, e.g. `koreanbeef`), `name`, `slots` (`breakfast`, `main`, `snack`), `cuisine`, `mins`, `prep`, `core`, `t: {L, S, X}` each `{k, p, c, f, cost, what}` plus optional `fib` (fibre g, only when it is large, e.g. Carb Balance wraps), `extras`, `steps`, and optional `batch`, `note` and `remake` (the ready meal it copies, e.g. `"YES-style chicken schnitzel"`; it puts the meal under the Remakes filter and in the ready-meal price comparison).
- **Hard rules (CI fails a meal that breaks them; `apps/android/app/src/test/js/meal-rules.test.js`):** kcal within 5% of 4P + 4C + 9F + 2×fib for every portion; Standard protein ≥ the lowest slot minimum (breakfast 30 g, main 45 g, snack 15 g); no banned food anywhere; complete shape (id, slots, prep, mins, all three portions, extras, at least 3 steps); every shopping amount a positive number or `{L, S, X}` with a portion > 0; steps never contain a gram or mL amount when the meal's amounts change by portion; batches complete and used only by meals that buy their item.
- **Guidelines (report them when proposing; never block):** kcal bands L 400–600, S 550–750, X 750–950; protein maximum breakfast 45 g, main 65 g, snack 25 g; cost over $7; prep over 20 min; beans; a product at only one store; ingredient reuse (products added that no meal uses yet — aim for at most one, never a perishable that would be left half-used — and which meals share its protein, carb and veg).

## Recipe standard

- Every shop ingredient has an amount for each portion in `catalogue.js` `AMOUNTS[meal]`: a number when it's the same for every portion, otherwise `{L, S, X}` (0 = that portion doesn't use it). Amounts must match each portion's `what` and macros.
- Non-shop ingredients go in the meal's `extras` with amounts: a string for every portion, or `{tiers: "SX", text}`.
- Steps are numbered, give heat and times, and say "the macaroni" rather than an amount that changes by portion.
- A meal that uses a batch lists `batch: ["chicken"]` and buys the raw item (150 g cooked chicken = 200 g raw breast at ratio 0.75).
- A batch in `BATCHES` has `id`, `name`, `item` (the catalogue item it's made from), `ratio` (cooked weight per raw weight, up to 1), `as` (how it reads in a recipe, e.g. `"Sunday chicken, cooked"`), `makes`, `keeps` (lowercase, reads after "Keeps") and `steps`.

## Products

- Name real products stocked at Coles or Woolworths and look them up on the live sites. Give exact product-page URLs, never search URLs; if there's no exact page, say so.
- Use label values where you can. Mark every price checked or estimate.
- Record products in `catalogue.js` `ITEMS` like the existing ones: item `id`, `sec` (0 daily fixtures, 1 fresh, 2 pantry and freezer), `name`, `unit`, `note`, `same`, and per store `name`, `url`, `price`, `size`, `checked` date (Woolworths also `stockcode`, which matches the URL; Coles URLs end with the product id).
- Add a new product's label macros to `MACROS` in `Weekday_Fuel.html`, or its shop row shows no macro line.
- Work portion figures out from the ingredient amounts and `MACROS` (add extras such as butter, mayo or avocado by hand), not by guessing. Set `cost` so Standard matches the catalogue's per-serve cost averaged over both stores.
- Label facts worth knowing: Mission Carb Balance wraps per 100 g 197 kcal, 13.4 g P, 9 g C, 4.8 g F, 34 g fibre (one wrap is 48 g); Tip Top English muffins per 100 g 227 kcal, 9.5 g P, 40.4 g C, 2.8 g F (one muffin 67 g); Gravox gravy powder per 100 g about 285 kcal (10 g makes ~100 mL); Chobani Natural Light per 100 g 56 kcal, 9.3 g P, 4 g C, 0.2 g F; Woolworths Light Tasty Shredded Cheese per 100 g 337 kcal, 29.7 g P, 24.2 g F; SunRice microwave pouch 250 g, 209 kcal per half pouch; Ayam Light Coconut Milk 172 kcal per 100 mL (use ~70 mL topped up with water).

## Where things go

- The meal object (portions, prep, batch, extras, steps, note) in `apps/android/app/src/main/assets/weekday_fuel/meals.js` `MEALS`; batch recipes in `BATCHES`.
- Shopping amounts in `catalogue.js` `AMOUNTS[meal]`, keyed by item id; new products as `ITEMS`.
- Meal ids never change (saved days, picks and orders use them).

## Workflow

1. Evan asks in chat, loosely or specifically.
2. Any needed food not in the food decisions list gets a yes/no question first, one food per question.
3. Propose each meal: cuisine, prep label and hands-on minutes, batch components, protein core, the L/S/X table (kcal, P, C, F, cost), ingredients with both stores' product links (checked/estimate), method, the ingredient-reuse report, guideline misses, assumptions and values you couldn't verify.
4. Evan approves, tweaks or rejects each meal.
5. Add approved meals to `meals.js` and `catalogue.js`, run `node --test "apps/android/app/src/test/js/*.test.js"`, check the Meals and Shop tabs in a browser, commit to `main`, wait for green CI, bump `versionCode` and `versionName` in `apps/android/app/build.gradle.kts`, commit and wait for the release.
6. Record food answers in the food decisions table.

Removing a meal: delete it from `MEALS` and `AMOUNTS`, and delete products no other meal uses from `ITEMS` and `MACROS` (`catalogue-data.test.js` fails on unused products). Check `Weekday_Fuel.html` for the id by name: `STARTER` (the starter Build a shop) and the `yogbowl` special cases in Suggest a day and the snack picker. The app ignores saved picks and day plans for unknown meals.
